Saint Clare of Assisi was a knight's daughter, raised for a splendid marriage. She wanted something else: to own nothing at all, like Christ, whose poor mother laid him in a manger. Years later, when the friars who begged for her sisters came home with whole loaves of bread, she would ask them, as if scolding, who had given them. Broken pieces made her happier. Her family fought her choice. In time, so did people who loved her and wanted her safe. What did that poverty mean to her, that she called it a great exchange? And what would it cost her to keep it?
